                    {
                        "translation_id": "77bcb0e1-8305-49ac-b8ab-8a3a00436a18",
                        "source_entry_id": "513623f5-b667-3aed-8e28-316d59836c27",
                        "source_sense_id": "b56796b4-532d-3ff9-bc05-01b63381ecee",
                        "target_lang_word": "inveterate",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "inveterate",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "01304d9a-ff5b-3235-a5b2-973848694b8e",
                                        "entry_id": "51547469-7eeb-34e8-bb05-408d3db384b8",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Firmly established from having been around for a long time; of long standing."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e72603f4-6888-403c-bce1-ca06f07e77ca",
                                                "sense_id": "01304d9a-ff5b-3235-a5b2-973848694b8e",
                                                "text": "Near-synonyms: deep-rooted, ingrained, ineradicable, indelible, radicated, chronic, permanent"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a2505f7c-891d-4f85-997c-dd2cd7b48133",
                                                "sense_id": "01304d9a-ff5b-3235-a5b2-973848694b8e",
                                                "text": "an inveterate disease"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "437942c0-b539-479b-9c35-7ac47ea14151",
                                                "sense_id": "01304d9a-ff5b-3235-a5b2-973848694b8e",
                                                "text": "an inveterate habit"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "41768850-b6c4-43cc-b78b-401a9571b479",
                                                "sense_id": "01304d9a-ff5b-3235-a5b2-973848694b8e",
                                                "text": "a Heaven's radiance of justice, prophetic, clearly of Heaven, discernible behind all these confused worldwide entanglements, of Landlord interests, Manufacturing interests, Tory-Whig interests, and who knows what other interests, expediencies, vested interests, established possessions, inveterate Dilettantisms, Midas-eared Mammonism."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "145666f6-ab07-4aea-9596-045309ad7724",
                                                "sense_id": "01304d9a-ff5b-3235-a5b2-973848694b8e",
                                                "text": "In Montpelier, where this prison stands, the inveterate prejudice against prisoners has been swept away."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "fc2d5ef7-ab12-3c61-bacc-8fc4b5fc0ab5",
                                        "entry_id": "51547469-7eeb-34e8-bb05-408d3db384b8",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Having had a habit (usually a bad habit) for a long time."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "of a person"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "89e07b72-458f-4078-ae8d-643f323f75b6",
                                                "sense_id": "fc2d5ef7-ab12-3c61-bacc-8fc4b5fc0ab5",
                                                "text": "Near-synonyms: hardened, chronic, dyed-in-the-wool"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ea284f23-0b34-4878-b66b-f27cb21dae87",
                                                "sense_id": "fc2d5ef7-ab12-3c61-bacc-8fc4b5fc0ab5",
                                                "text": "an inveterate idler;   an inveterate gambler;   an inveterate smoker"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "4e673cfc-e98d-4e49-b39d-03de5e9db8a5",
                                                "sense_id": "fc2d5ef7-ab12-3c61-bacc-8fc4b5fc0ab5",
                                                "text": "an inveterate traveller"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e82c241d-762e-49dd-bbeb-73ca8da78aa1",
                                                "sense_id": "fc2d5ef7-ab12-3c61-bacc-8fc4b5fc0ab5",
                                                "text": "\"Say no more,\" interrupted Henrietta: \"the very mention of that inveterate gossip accounts for every thing. Do let me, my dear Mrs. Courtenaye,\" and she took her hand with a kindness that was irresistible, \"let me warn you against allowing your happiness to be the sport of a woman like that;..."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3e2d6960-1a5d-466e-b761-f1e6a39f66f9",
                                                "sense_id": "fc2d5ef7-ab12-3c61-bacc-8fc4b5fc0ab5",
                                                "text": "[S]he offered kisses to a stranger so confidingly that the most inveterate bachelor relented."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a5819321-1f82-4fd5-844e-da786d829de1",
                                                "sense_id": "fc2d5ef7-ab12-3c61-bacc-8fc4b5fc0ab5",
                                                "text": "Like many lonely people, he was an inveterate hoarder, making and surrounding himself with objects, barriers against the demands of human intimacy."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "3d8c42de-a76d-32ed-b296-b762db7c1c2b",
                                        "entry_id": "51547469-7eeb-34e8-bb05-408d3db384b8",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Malignant; virulent; spiteful."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "53248002-bca5-476e-9b11-38f68dcffd0c",
                                                "sense_id": "3d8c42de-a76d-32ed-b296-b762db7c1c2b",
                                                "text": "A man of mild manners can form no idea of inveterate revenge or cruelty[…]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "5d2ced9e-5f60-4a33-8f2e-264e15caac40",
                                                "sense_id": "3d8c42de-a76d-32ed-b296-b762db7c1c2b",
                                                "text": "1765–70, Henry Brooke, The Fool of Quality; or, The History of Henry, Earl of Moreland\nThis his lordship perused with a countenance, and scrutiny, apparently inveterate."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "inveterate",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "89c05c03-e6de-334c-9c86-aaca90e3079f",
                                        "entry_id": "9dd49e6a-9f2c-38b3-b99a-539ed3747db2",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To fix and settle after a long time; to entrench."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "obsolete"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7f8e24a4-4309-445b-9535-04cc2e3c41ca",
                                                "sense_id": "89c05c03-e6de-334c-9c86-aaca90e3079f",
                                                "text": "\"the vulgar conceived that now there was an end given, and a consummation to superstitious prophecies, the belief of fools, but the talk sometimes of wise men, and to an ancient tacit expectation which had by tradition been infused and inveterated into men's minds.\""
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "859e0580-6f92-4401-82e6-db862a961f9f",
                                                "sense_id": "89c05c03-e6de-334c-9c86-aaca90e3079f",
                                                "text": "\"none of these Princes do use to maintaine any armies together, which are annex'd and inveterated with the governments of the provinces, as were the armies of the Roman Empire. \""
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0c0d2611-9c17-4d51-9b78-b59e23ee074f",
                                                "sense_id": "89c05c03-e6de-334c-9c86-aaca90e3079f",
                                                "text": "The foregoing elements of disunion are inveterated by the constituent formation of our national legislature. In the French chambers the members are all Frenchmen ; but our members of Congress are effectively Georgians, New-Yorkers, Carolinians, Pennsylvanians, &c."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "ed749b74-7750-4828-8d1b-3ee882915eba",
                        "source_entry_id": "513623f5-b667-3aed-8e28-316d59836c27",
                        "source_sense_id": "b56796b4-532d-3ff9-bc05-01b63381ecee",
                        "target_lang_word": "dyed-in-the-wool",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "dyed-in-the-wool",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "ff8aad12-fac4-343e-aeff-7e963d5d2647",
                                        "entry_id": "f666d483-4147-3236-94e1-a77195748e35",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Having the fibres dyed before they are formed into cloth."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "of textiles"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b61b488c-1568-4854-8956-4978a8ac0578",
                                                "sense_id": "ff8aad12-fac4-343e-aeff-7e963d5d2647",
                                                "text": "[…] some Druggets with Thread, that are dyed in the Piece; the others are dyed in the Wool, i. e. the Wool of which they are made, is dyed of several Colours, before it is carded, spun, and weaved."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "23e563bd-d3dc-44cb-a2ec-5b47547a05f9",
                                                "sense_id": "ff8aad12-fac4-343e-aeff-7e963d5d2647",
                                                "text": "That cloth will keep its colour best that is dyed in the wool; and the vessel will longest retain the scent of that liquor with which it is first seasoned."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "e0846f7d-90f4-3543-af86-26a05471fb5c",
                                        "entry_id": "f666d483-4147-3236-94e1-a77195748e35",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Firmly established in one's beliefs or habits; having a specified characteristic, identity, etc. deeply ingrained in one's nature."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "figuratively",
                                            "idiomatic"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "640dfb3d-7cb5-421e-9191-eceabc7acc22",
                                                "sense_id": "e0846f7d-90f4-3543-af86-26a05471fb5c",
                                                "text": "Smith was a dyed-in-the-wool typist and never really got used to writing on computers."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "16e622bb-5a89-4843-88fd-cf3a860885f7",
                                                "sense_id": "e0846f7d-90f4-3543-af86-26a05471fb5c",
                                                "text": "John Major was described by his opponents as a dyed-in-the-wool Conservative."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c0555a8d-b1b2-49ad-9533-9d244ab1016a",
                                                "sense_id": "e0846f7d-90f4-3543-af86-26a05471fb5c",
                                                "text": "We all know a process, sir, by which the whole Essex Junto could, in one hour, be all washed white from their ancient federalism, and come out, every one of them, an original democrat, dyed in the wool!"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "bc6b9b04-acd9-477f-860c-380d40123af6",
                                                "sense_id": "e0846f7d-90f4-3543-af86-26a05471fb5c",
                                                "text": "He might truly be termed a legitimate son of the revenue system, dyed in the wool, or rather, born in the purple; since his sire, a Revolutionary colonel, and formerly collector of the port, had created an office for him, and appointed him to fill it, at a period of the early ages which few living men can now remember."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7d31e819-9b47-401f-b74c-d15e6d566de4",
                                                "sense_id": "e0846f7d-90f4-3543-af86-26a05471fb5c",
                                                "text": "Although appreciating the rapidity and frequency of the Southern electric services I was now to use on short journeys, I became more than ever convinced that electric traction offers very little of interest to the dyed-in-the-wool railwayist."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0a3c9628-d198-446d-9c5c-5d5923743d6a",
                                                "sense_id": "e0846f7d-90f4-3543-af86-26a05471fb5c",
                                                "text": "For all he says he isn’t, he’s a bit of an ultramontane, in practice though not in theory, and we can’t have that in the Church of England, we must stay dyed-in-the-wool Anglican."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c48ce176-84c7-4148-a034-8c159c929270",
                                                "sense_id": "e0846f7d-90f4-3543-af86-26a05471fb5c",
                                                "text": "Our president is no lover of Jews and more than likely a dyed-in-the-wool anti-Semite […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "6ccb6833-c894-4e8b-951e-d456f1455c95",
                                                "sense_id": "e0846f7d-90f4-3543-af86-26a05471fb5c",
                                                "text": "Again, to go back to the history of British Railways, there were moves to introduce electrification more widely when the West Coast Main Line was sparked up in the 1960s, but this was rejected by dyed-in-the-wool old regional railway managers who did not like the hassle of putting up the wires."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
